{
  "dom_id": "step-q142068",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/d8d2a012db4de4a2d9a008bae9d7dae854eb6071e0c5da6d1e65eea6b93f8c97.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0?$",
  "worked_solution": "The derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ is positive when $f(x)$ is increasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=c$ are on increasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/be95fe97afc8c90a372176e3dcf19fe49493d0e92059e7ceb5a4a40e77f23ee6.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\u2323$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\u2322$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=c,$ the graph is concave up ($\u2323$) at $x=b$ only.",
